Suppose there are vertices $u_H,v_H\in V$ such that $$\label{hc:witness}
 \|X_{u_H}-x_H\|_1\le a_H,
 \qquad \|X_{v_H}-x_H\|_1\ge b_H.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
There is an absolute integer $q_H\ge2$ such that the following holds for each integer $q\ge q_H$. Let $H$ have a locally $q$-connected hierarchy, and let $\mathcal M$ be any collection of its nonroot nodes satisfying $|O(A)|\ge |P(A)|/q$. Then $H$ has a shortcut matrix $D$ such that $$\label{eq:hierarchy-power}
 \frac{1}{|O(A)|}\sum_{e\in O(A)}R_D(e)\le q^{-31/40}
 \qquad(A\in\mathcal M).$$ The cutoff is independent of the graph, the hierarchy depth and the marked collection $\mathcal M$.

With the preceding notation, set $$\label{hd:ratio}
 Q_*=
 \sup_{\substack{h\geq m,\ X\in\{0,1\}^{h\times V},\ \mathcal E(X)>0\\
                  U\in\mathbb R^{E\times h},\ UU^{\mathsf T}=I_m}}
 \frac{\displaystyle
       \sum_{t\in\mathcal M}\frac1{|O(t)|}
       \left(\sum_{e\in O(t)}\langle u_e,Xb_e\rangle\right)^2}
      {\mathcal E(X)}.$$ Then $$\label{hd:comparison}
 p_*\leq Q_*\leq2p_*.$$ Consequently, if every ratio in <a href="#hd:ratio" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="hd:ratio">[hd:ratio]</a> is at most $K$, then for every $\eta>0$ there is a full positive definite matrix $D$ satisfying all the cut constraints in <a href="#hd:primal" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="hd:primal">[hd:primal]</a> and all the marked-node average-resistance bounds with right side $K+\eta$.

Let $F$ be a nonempty set of oriented edges of a finite multigraph with edge set $E$, and put $m=|F|$. For points $Y_v\in\mathbb R^E$, write $Y_e=Y_u-Y_v$ when $e=(u,v)$, and denote the $e$-coordinate of $Y_e$ by $Y_{e,e}$. Suppose that $a>0$ and $$\label{hb:spectral-assumption}
 \Upsilon:=\left(\frac1m\sum_{e\in F}Y_{e,e}\right)^2>0,
 \qquad
 \Upsilon\ge\frac a m\sum_{e\in F}\|Y_e\|_2^2.$$ Then $a\le1$, and there are $b\ge2$ pairwise disjoint Euclidean balls of one positive squared radius $r$, centered at images of endpoints of edges in $F$, such that $$\label{hb:spectral-logarithmic}
 b\ge\frac{am}{16},
 \qquad
 br\ge\frac{9m\Upsilon}
 {512\bigl(5+\log_2(1/a)\bigr)^2}.$$ Consequently, for every $0<\varepsilon<1/3$, the choice $$\label{hb:spectral-constant}
 C_1(\varepsilon)=\frac{1024}{\varepsilon^2}$$ gives $$\label{hb:spectral-conclusion}
 b\ge\frac{a|F|}{C_1(\varepsilon)},
 \qquad
 br\ge\frac{a^\varepsilon\Upsilon|F|}{C_1(\varepsilon)}.$$

Let $T$ be a nonempty set of $\alpha$-bad nodes with positive boundary sums, and let $0<\gamma\le1$. Suppose that $O'(t)$ is $\gamma$-dominating for each $t\in T$ and that $F=\bigcup_{t\in T}O'(t)$ is $4$-homogeneous. Write $$s=\frac{\alpha\gamma}{32},\qquad
 c_1=\min_{e\in F}a_e^2,
 \qquad
 S'=\sum_{t\in T}\frac1{|O(t)|}
                     \left(\sum_{e\in O'(t)}a_e\right)^2.$$ For $\beta>1$ and $0<\epsilon<1/3$, put $C=C_1(\epsilon)=1024\epsilon^{-2}$. If $$\label{hb:smallness}
 s^\epsilon\le\frac1{48\beta C},$$ there is either a family of $\beta$-compact bags or a family of $s^{1+2\epsilon}$-assigned bags, assigned to a subset of $T$, whose mass is at least $$\label{hb:one-mass}
 \frac{s^\epsilon}{192\beta C}\,S'.$$ Its radius, and also its diameter parameter in the compact case, belong to $[sc_1,12c_1/s]$.

Let $T$ be a nonempty finite set of $\alpha$-bad nodes with positive boundary sums, where $0<\alpha\le1$. Define $$\begin{aligned}
 J&=1+\lceil\log_2(256/\alpha^2)\rceil,
 &C_2(\alpha)&=128J^4,
 &s&=\alpha/C_2(\alpha),\\
 L&=2+\lceil\log_2(1/s)\rceil,
 &H&=1+\lceil\log_2(24/(\lambda s^2))\rceil,
 &C_1(\epsilon)&=1024\epsilon^{-2}.
 \end{aligned}$$ Suppose $\beta>1$, $0<\epsilon<1/3$, $0<\lambda<1$, and $s^\epsilon\le(48\beta C_1(\epsilon))^{-1}$. There is a $\lambda$-geometric sequence of families, consisting either entirely of $\beta$-compact bags or entirely of $s^{1+2\epsilon}$-assigned bags assigned to distinct nodes of $T$, whose mass satisfies $$\label{hb:extraction-bound}
 \mathsf M\ge
 \frac{s^\epsilon}{384\beta C_1(\epsilon)C_2(\alpha)LH}
 \sum_{t\in T}\frac1{|O(t)|}
                  \left(\sum_{e\in O(t)}a_e\right)^2.$$ In particular, all losses are functions of $\alpha,\epsilon,\beta$ and $\lambda$, independent of the number of vertices and hierarchy depth.

Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite $k$-edge-connected multigraph, where $k>0$, and let $X_v\in Q=[0,1]^h$ for $v\in V$. Let $\mathcal Z$ be a finite collection of pairwise disjoint ordinary or hollowed balls in $Q$. For each $H\in\mathcal Z$, write $x_H,a_H,b_H$ for its center and numerical inner and outer radii. Suppose there are vertices $u_H,v_H\in V$ such that $$\label{hc:witness}
 \|X_{u_H}-x_H\|_1\le a_H,
 \qquad \|X_{v_H}-x_H\|_1\ge b_H.$$ Then $$\label{hc:length-bound}
 k\sum_{H\in\mathcal Z}(b_H-a_H)
 \le \sum_{\{u,v\}\in E}\|X_u-X_v\|_1.$$

Let $X:V\to\{0,1\}^{d}$, and let $\mathcal T$ be a locally $k$-connected hierarchy of $G$, with selected nonroot nodes $T$ satisfying $|O(t)|\ge k\lambda|P(t)|$ for $t\in T$. Suppose $\mathcal F_1,\ldots,\mathcal F_m$ is a $\lambda$-geometric sequence of families of $(24C_3/k)$-assigned bags, of types $(\delta_i,T_i)$, with $T_i\subseteq T$. If $$C_4\ge3,\qquad \lambda\le\frac1{6C_4},\qquad
 C_3\ge2\bigl((C_4+1)+4(C_4+2)^2\bigr),$$ then $$\label{ha:assigned-bound}
 \sum_{\{u,v\}\in E}\|X_u-X_v\|_1
 \ \ge\ \frac{k}{8}\frac{C_4}{12C_3}
 \sum_{i=1}^m\delta_i\sum_{t\in T_i}|\operatorname{Bag}_t|.$$ In particular, one may take $C_4=3$, $C_3=10^4$ and $\lambda\le1/18$.

Let $\mathcal T$ be a locally $k$-connected hierarchy, with selected nonroot nodes $T$ satisfying $|O(t)|\ge k\lambda|P(t)|$ for $t\in T$. Let $\mathcal F_i$ be a $\lambda$-geometric sequence of families of $12C_3/k$-assigned bags of type $(\delta_i,T_i)$, where the $T_i\subseteq T$ are pairwise disjoint. Suppose all input balls are insertable and $$C_4\ge3,\qquad \lambda\le\frac{1}{6C_4},\qquad
 C_3\ge2\bigl((C_4+1)+4(C_4+2)^2\bigr).$$ Then there is a finite valid collection $Z$ of labeled ordinary and hollow balls, with centers inherited from the input balls, such that $$\label{ha:arrangement-width}
 \sum_{B\in Z}\operatorname{width}(B)
 \ge \frac{C_4}{12C_3}
       \sum_i\delta_i\sum_{t\in T_i}|\operatorname{Bag}_t|.$$
